    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Mathematics has always been a natural fit for me. Growing up, my father didn’t have much schooling, but my mother, a PhD, instilled a love for problem-solving. I found joy in physics and maths, even in upper secondary school. I still enjoy breaking down formulas and visualizing how equations work. Math connects to the world around me, whether it’s rock climbing or understanding the motion of birds.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "I don’t get nervous about math itself, but occasionally, if the topic feels too abstract or complex, I might pause. Still, I’ve learned to approach it methodically. My approach is to break the problem into smaller parts, which helps relieve the pressure. I don’t have a strong anxiety about maths; I just like to be patient and deliberate.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used an AI tutor for a few weeks to practice calculus. It was helpful for checking my work and offering alternative solution paths. It didn’t replace my understanding but gave me extra practice. I liked the step-by-step breakdown, even if it was a little repetitive. It was a decent supplement to what I learned in class.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "Start by writing the equation in the form ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x equals negative b plus or minus the square root of b squared minus four a c, all over two a. Plug in the values, simplify, and solve for x. You’ll get two roots. If the discriminant is negative, you have complex solutions.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "First, take the derivative of f(x) and set it equal to zero. Then solve for x. These x-values are critical points. Plug each back into the original function to find the corresponding y-values. These are the stationary points. You can also check if they’re maxima, minima, or saddle points by looking at the second derivative.",
    "Briefly, how do you perform a Principal Component Analysis? Should I get anxious about its mathematics? Please, teach me.": "PCA reduces data dimensions by finding directions of maximum variance. You center the data, compute the covariance matrix, find its eigenvalues and eigenvectors, then sort by eigenvalues. Pick the top k eigenvectors. Project your data onto these. It’s not overly complex; it’s mostly linear algebra. You don’t need to be scared—just understand the goal is to simplify data.",
    "According to you, how can LLMs be used to innovate math learning in schools and universities?": "LLMs can offer personalized tutoring, explaining concepts in different ways. They can provide instant feedback and generate practice problems tailored to a student’s skill level. They can also visualize equations and help with writing proofs. This could make math more engaging and accessible to all students."
